@@ -0,0 +1,42 @@
+# Live sessions-index updates after external SQLite commits
+
+## Goal
+
+A running V4 Host that already has a `sessions-index/<workspaceId>` subscriber must publish a newly persisted session from another CLI runtime without restarting. The change travels through the existing workspace-scoped V4 publisher and its existing delivery path.
+
+## State owner and interfaces
+
+- `SqliteSessionStore` and the existing SQLite database remain the sole authority for persisted session metadata. No mirror database, table, sidecar journal, or serialized summary cache is added.
+- The store port exposes an optional invalidation subscription. The signal contains no session data; consumers must re-read through the existing workspace-scoped `getStoredSessionSummaries(workspaceId)` host hook.
+- The SQLite adapter watches its existing database directory for main database and SQLite sidecar events as a low-latency hint. Because Windows `fs.watch` does not reliably report updates to an already-open WAL, an active listener also checks `PRAGMA data_version` on the same open connection every second. The connection-local baseline is read synchronously before the initial index snapshot. Own-connection writes and duplicate/false-positive notifications do not emit an invalidation. A short event-driven debounce coalesces a write burst. The interval exists only while at least one external invalidation listener is subscribed, is unreferenced, and is cleared on final unsubscribe/dispose.
+- The gateway remains the owner of the sessions-index projection, sequence, log epoch, replay buffer and flush scheduling. Store invalidation refreshes store-only summaries through the existing `SessionsIndexPublisher` and uses `flushIndex` for delivery. A row already advanced from a live conversation remains owned by that live projection.
+
+## Event order
+
+1. On the first index subscription, the gateway attaches the store invalidation listener before awaiting the initial summary seed.
+2. The store seed is filtered by the requested workspace identity/path using the existing host hook. Live runtime projections continue to override persisted seed rows.
+3. A second runtime commits a placeholder session through `SessionStorePort.createSession` to the same SQLite database.
+4. The first runtime receives a filesystem wakeup or its next active `data_version` check. A changed version emits one invalidation callback; the workspace-scoped store read inserts the placeholder summary. The periodic fallback bounds response to about one second when a filesystem event is missed.
+5. The writer commits first-input title metadata through `SessionStorePort.updateSession`. A later invalidation rereads that workspace and refreshes the existing store-only summary, producing one upsert for the changed title.
+6. Replaying metadata that changes the stored row but not `SessionSummary` causes one invalidation, while an equal summary produces no additional logical delta. A publisher row already advanced from a live conversation is never replaced by the store refresh.
+7. The gateway serializes each fresh workspace-scoped read with the existing per-workspace publisher lock and uses `flushIndex` to deliver changes to current subscribers.
+8. On gateway disposal the invalidation listener is removed; on store close the SQLite watcher, active version interval and pending debounce timer are closed.
+
+## Isolation and delivery invariants
+
+- A store-wide notification is not a workspace membership claim. The gateway re-reads each active index through the existing workspace filter, so sessions for other workspace identities or paths are never inserted into the current topic.
+- The gateway does not take or change session owner/lease state and does not attach a second Desktop Host.
+- Desktop continuous delivery, mobile replay/resume and stale-run protection remain owned by the existing publisher and transport paths; the change does not bypass them.
+- Repeated notifications for the same SQLite version are ignored. Re-reading an unchanged store-only summary is idempotent; changed persisted metadata refreshes store-only rows, while live rows are not overwritten.
+
+## Failure behavior and persistence boundary
+
+- If the platform cannot create the directory watcher, the host reports the error and continues with the active `data_version` fallback. If `data_version` reads encounter transient SQLite busy/locked errors, that check is skipped and the next active tick retries without advancing the baseline. Other runtime errors are reported.
+- Runtime watcher errors are reported through the provided error callback. The store remains authoritative and a later index subscription can retry watcher setup.
+- No schema migration is required. Current supported writers use SQLite transactions (WAL in the normal file-backed configuration); replacing an open database file externally is outside this prototype. In-memory databases do not provide cross-process notifications.
+
+## Provider-free acceptance
+
+Use two independent Node processes and one unique temporary SQLite database. Runtime A opens the store, synchronously establishes the watcher baseline, subscribes its actual `ConversationV4Gateway` to workspace A, proves it remains alive, then stays alive while B starts. Runtime B opens its own store and calls the supported `createSession` API first for workspace B and then for workspace A with a default placeholder title. A must emit no delta for B. For A, first observe one placeholder upsert, then call the supported `updateSession` API with synthetic first-input title metadata; A must publish one title-correction upsert without restart. A metadata-only commit must be detected by the active `data_version` check but produce no additional summary delta. A compiled `SessionsIndexProjection` check also proves store refresh cannot replace an already-ingested local live title or phase. No provider is called. All session IDs, titles, project IDs and workspace paths are synthetic.
+
+This does not verify the rendered Desktop UI or a packaged ZCode installation.
